WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.150
Real-world objects are
fairly complicated.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.840
Often, their virtual
representations

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.730
are greatly simplified.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.370
When we compare this table
model from Google Warehouse

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.680
to its real-world
counterpart, we immediately

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.249
notice that many
details are missing.

00:00:17.249 --> 00:00:18.790
These details are
what make designing

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:22.930
fabricable objects very
difficult for casual users.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.030
In this work, we
present a modeling tool

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.980
that leverages information
from a collection

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.220
of fabricable designs, in order
to enable casual users to model

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.509
and build real-world objects.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.780
To allow structure-preserving
manipulations,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.640
we automatically
convert the designs

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.140
in our collection
to hierarchical

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.240
parameterized templates.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.120
Our template model takes into
account global relationships,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.420
such as symmetries and
articulation consistencies.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.330
It is also not restricted to
fixed topologies, allowing

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.190
irregular patterns
to be rescaled.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.310
Notice how the number and
position of connectors

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.700
changes, as the template
parameters vary.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.220
Our modeling tool is based
on template manipulation

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:03.750
and composition.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.489
We allow users to
explore the space

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.710
of template variations
using different levels

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.340
of the template hierarchy.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.150
During manipulation, active
elements of the hierarchy

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.430
are shown in full color,
while other elements

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.070
are semitransparent.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.910
Once a part is
positioned, the user

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.280
invokes a method that brings
in connecting elements

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.160
to the design, by exploring
the template database.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.010
Notice how for our
a wooden tabletop,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.020
corner brackets are
automatically brought in

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:29.650
to connect the top to the legs.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.730
While, for a glass stop, a whole
support structure for the glass

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.470
is automatically added.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.890
We incorporate in our system a
stability analysis evaluation

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.380
that warns the user if the
design model is unstable.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.510
We illustrate a full
modeling session.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.640
For composition, we
first assist the users

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.490
by snapping drag components
onto the working model.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.710
The user can continue to
reposition and rescale

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.570
the model, until
she is satisfied

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.243
with the snap configuration.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.990
The output of our system
is a comprehensive bill

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.900
of materials that can be
directly used for fabrication.

00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:12.210
Here are some
additional results.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.530
One of the main advantages of
using a data-driven approach

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.150
is its generality.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.940
Notice how the same method that
creates tables and cabinets

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.500
can also be used on go-karts.

